{"step_index":2192,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T03:16:31Z","thinking":"**Designing High-Performance Rust**\n\nI'm now focusing on designing `main.rs` for the hexological engine, aiming for high performance. My current approach involves 18,432-dimensional hexological vector nodes, each consuming 72 KB of memory. I'm exploring how to efficiently represent and manage these nodes within the Rust program to maximize performance.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"//! Sovereign 18,432-Dimensional Hexological Vectoring Engine (Rust 1.95+)\n//! Substrate: Apple Silicon M4 Bare Metal (10 CPU Cores, Rayon Multithreading)\n//!\n//! Architecture:\n//! - 18,432 Float32 Dimensions per Memory Node (6 Faces x 3,072-D or 24 Heads x 768-D)\n//! - Memory Footprint: 73,728 Bytes (72 KB) per Node\n//! - SIMD Kernel: Auto-vectorized 4-lane / 16-lane FMA loops\n//! - Multithreading: Rayon Parallel Iterator across 10 M4 CPU Cores\n\nuse chrono::Utc;\nuse rayon::prelude::*;\nuse serde::Serialize;\nuse std::fs::File;\nuse std::io::Write;\nuse std::time::Instant;\n\nconst DIM: usize = 18432;\nconst TOTAL_VECTORS: usize = 100_000; // 100,000 18,432-D Node Evaluations (7.37 GB Vector Data)\n\n#[derive(Serialize)]\nstruct AuditReport {\n    timestamp: String,\n    engine: String,\n    rustc_version: String,\n    total_vectors: usize,\n    vector_dim: usize,\n    bytes_processed_gb: f64,\n    elapsed_ms: f64,\n    evaluations_per_sec: f64,\n    gflops: f